# ===========================================================================
# PUBLIC — safe to ship to the browser (Next.js exposes anything prefixed
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ client-side). Never put a real secret behind this prefix.
# ===========================================================================

# Supabase project — used for Auth (login/register/session) and Storage
# (private per-user document/lecture-slide uploads). The anon/publishable
# key is NOT secret (Supabase designs it to be shipped to the browser); it
# only works within whatever your RLS policies allow.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="sb_publishable_..."

# ===========================================================================
# SERVER-ONLY SECRETS — never prefix these with NEXT_PUBLIC_, never send
# them to the browser. In Vercel, add these under Project Settings →
# Environment Variables (they're server-only there by default — only
# NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed vars ever reach client code).
#
# Both database URLs below embed the database password, so they belong in
# this section and not the public one above.
# ===========================================================================

# The connection the running app uses for every query. Copy it from the
# Supabase dashboard → Connect → "Transaction pooler"; do not hand-assemble
# the host, since the region and pooler generation differ per project.
#
# Two things about it matter for performance, and both are easy to get
# wrong:
#
#  * It must be the pooler host on port 6543, not the direct
#    db.<project-ref>.supabase.co host on 5432. Serverless functions start
#    cold constantly, and against the direct host every cold start pays a
#    fresh TCP handshake, TLS handshake and Postgres startup before the
#    first row is read. The direct host is also IPv6-only and unreachable
#    from most serverless platforms, Vercel included.
#
#  * `pgbouncer=true` is required. The transaction pooler hands a different
#    backend connection to each statement, so Prisma must stop relying on
#    server-side prepared statements. Omitting this produces intermittent
#    "prepared statement already exists" errors under concurrency.
#
# connection_limit sizes Prisma's own client-side pool, per function
# instance. Do NOT set this to 1 here.
#
# The "use connection_limit=1 in serverless" advice is about connecting
# straight to Postgres, where each client connection is a real backend
# process and the database's max_connections is the scarce resource. Behind
# the transaction pooler that is no longer the shape of the problem:
# Supavisor multiplexes many client connections onto few real backends, so
# holding several is cheap.
#
# What is expensive here is serialising. The dashboard alone fans out to
# roughly 25 queries through nested Promise.all (the priority engine, the
# academic-health score and the dashboard's own batch). With a limit of 1
# they all queue behind a single connection; exceed Prisma's pool_timeout
# (10s by default) and every one of them fails with P2024, which surfaces
# as a 500 on the homepage rather than a slow page.
#
# 10 is a sane value for this app: comfortably above the widest fan-out
# without being an unreasonable number of client slots to hold.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@<pooler-host>:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=10"

# Used ONLY by Prisma's schema tooling (`prisma migrate`, `prisma db pull`,
# `prisma studio`) — never by the running app, and never on a request path.
# Those commands need a session-mode connection, which the transaction
# pooler above cannot provide.
#
# Copy the Supabase dashboard's "Session pooler" string (the pooler host on
# port 5432) rather than the direct connection: it is session-mode as
# required, and unlike the direct host it is reachable over IPv4, so it also
# works from CI and from machines without IPv6. Use the direct
# db.<project-ref>.supabase.co:5432 string instead only if you know your
# environment has IPv6.
#
# Leaving this unset is fine if you apply schema changes through the
# Supabase SQL editor, which is what this project does — `prisma generate`
# and `next build` both succeed without it.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@<pooler-host>:5432/postgres"

# Required ONLY for the background file-processing job
# (src/app/api/cron/process-documents/route.ts). That job runs outside any
# user's session — triggered by Vercel Cron, not a logged-in request — so
# it can't use a per-user access token like the rest of the app; it needs
# the service role key, which bypasses Storage RLS by design. Get it from
# Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role.
# Without it, uploaded documents stay QUEUED (never processed, never
# incorrectly marked FAILED) until this is set.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="eyJ..."

# Authenticates Vercel's own cron trigger against the processing route —
# NOT a Supabase credential. Vercel automatically sends this as
# "Authorization: Bearer <value>" on every request it makes to a
# vercel.json cron path when this env var is set; the route rejects any
# request whose bearer token doesn't match. Generate any long random
# string (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`) and set the same value in Vercel.
CRON_SECRET="a-long-random-string"
